<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-american-civil-war">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between 1861 and 1865 the United States fought a war that killed more Americans than any
    other conflict in its history — and the question this page answers is the one the
    syllabus asks in four moves: <strong>how far did slavery cause it, why did Lincoln's
    election matter, why did the North win, and did the war change anything?</strong> The
    short version: a nation half slave and half free could keep the peace only while there
    was unclaimed territory left to argue about. The compromises of 1820–1850 bought time by
    splitting the difference; when the land ran out in the 1850s, the differences had to be
    settled — and one side had stopped believing the other could be trusted with any
    settlement at all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from compromise to catastrophe, 1820–77 — filter by phase with the chips" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <h2>How far did slavery cause the war?</h2>
  <p>
    Slavery was not a moral stain bolted onto an otherwise healthy economy — it <em>was</em>
    the South's economy. After Eli Whitney's cotton gin (1793) made short-staple cotton
    profitable, cotton grown by enslaved labour became the United States' greatest export,
    worth over half of all US export earnings by 1860 and demanding constant new land, because
    cotton exhausts soil within a decade. The 3.95 million people enumerated as enslaved in
    the 1860 census were property worth something like $3 billion — more than all the
    country's factories and railways combined — so the South defended the institution with
    everything it had, and demanded that the federal government defend it too. The North,
    industrialising on free labour, grew morally opposed as abolitionists — Garrison's
    <em>The Liberator</em> (1831), the escaped slave turned orator Frederick Douglass, and
    Harriet Beecher Stowe's <em>Uncle Tom's Cabin</em> (1852), which sold 300,000 copies in a
    year — made slavery a national conscience issue.
  </p>
  <p>
    The quarrel was manageable only while there was new territory to bargain over, and each
    compromise managed it and then collapsed. The Missouri Compromise (1820) drew the line at
    36°30′; the Compromise of 1850 admitted California free but gave the South a Fugitive
    Slave Act so ferocious that northern citizens were legally obliged to help hunt runaway
    people in Boston; the Kansas-Nebraska Act (1854) let settlers vote slavery up or down and
    produced "Bleeding Kansas", small-scale civil war that killed the Whig Party and created
    the Republican Party as the explicit anti-expansion party; and the Dred Scott decision
    (1857) ruled that Congress had never been allowed to ban slavery from any territory —
    voiding every compromise retroactively. By 1858 Lincoln could argue the nation could not
    permanently endure "half slave and half free"; by 1859 John Brown had tried to start the
    uprising himself. Each crisis made the other section look less like a partner and more
    like an enemy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the war was really about states' rights, with slavery as a
    sideshow. The seceding states' own declarations demolish this: South Carolina's
    declaration of December 1860 cites the election of a president "whose opinions and
    purposes are hostile to slavery" as its leading grievance, and the Confederate
    vice-president Alexander Stephens said in March 1861 that slavery was the new nation's
    "cornerstone". States' rights was the vehicle; slavery was the destination — and the
    Confederacy's constitution actually <em>restricted</em> its states' rights to abolish
    slavery. Equally, the North's war aim at the start was not abolition but the restoration
    of the Union: Congress voted in July 1861 that it was fighting to preserve the Union, not
    to interfere with slavery where it legally existed.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the enslaved population of the United States at each census, 1790–1860" widget="plotter" config={censusCfg} />

  <p>
    The curve is the stakes. From just under 700,000 in 1790 to nearly four million by 1860 —
    growing about 30 per cent per decade, almost entirely through natural increase — it made
    slavery simultaneously the South's labour system, property regime and social order. The
    political fights of 1820–57 were arguments about this curve's future: <em>where could it
    legally grow?</em> Free-soil politics said: into no new territory, so that it would die
    hemmed in, as the founders had assumed it would.
  </p>

  <h2>What was the significance of Lincoln's election?</h2>
  <p>
    Lincoln won on 6 November 1860 with under 40 per cent of the popular vote, appeared on
    the ballot in none of ten southern states, and carried virtually every free state — a
    purely sectional victory. That was the significance: for the first time a president whose
    party existed to stop slavery's expansion would appoint the officials, judges and postmasters
    of the territories. His platform said nothing about abolishing slavery where it existed —
    and he repeated that promise — but the South had spent thirty years watching the
    arithmetic: the free states were growing faster, and if the Senate ever turned
    anti-slavery, the institution would be immortal nowhere. Secession began before he even
    took office: South Carolina left on 20 December 1860, six states followed, and in
    February 1861 they formed the Confederate States of America under Jefferson Davis. When
    Lincoln resupplied the federal garrison at Fort Sumter in Charleston harbour, Confederate
    guns fired on it on 12 April 1861 — the North now had a flag to rally round — and Lincoln
    called for 75,000 volunteers. Four more slave states, led by Virginia, seceded rather
    than march against their neighbours. The election had not started the war; it had removed
    the last reason for the South to stay.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Lincoln went to war to free the slaves. In 1861 his aim was
    the Union; he even revoked an early general's attempt to free enslaved people in Missouri.
    Emancipation came two years in, as a <em>war measure</em> — and as we will see, it
    transformed the war itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Why was the North able to win the war?</h2>
  <p>
    The South's task was easier in theory — survive long enough to be recognised — and
    hopeless in the arithmetic. The Union held about 22 million people against the
    Confederacy's 9 million (of whom nearly 4 million were enslaved and watched closely); it
    manufactured the overwhelming majority of the country's firearms and cloth, owned over
    two-thirds of the railway mileage, and had virtually the whole navy. General Winfield
    Scott's "Anaconda Plan" — blockade the coast, squeeze down the Mississippi — looked
    slow, and was; but it worked. The blockade strangled cotton exports (the Confederacy's
    only source of foreign credit) and imports of weapons, rails and medicine. "King Cotton
    diplomacy" failed: Britain, the great textile power, had a cotton surplus in 1861, developed
    Egypt and India as suppliers, and would not intervene for slavery after the
    Emancipation Proclamation made the war explicitly anti-slave.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turning weight into victory took time and blood. The first years went badly for the Union
    (Bull Run, Fredericksburg, Chancellorsville), because the North lacked generals willing to
    use its advantages. Note too what did <em>not</em> happen: the four slave states that
    stayed in the Union — Maryland, Missouri, Kentucky and Delaware — were Lincoln's quiet
    triumph. Losing Maryland would have ringed Washington with Confederate territory and
    probably ended the war; he held them by a blend of firmness (suspending habeas corpus in
    Maryland) and restraint (leaving slavery legal there until the 13th Amendment).
    <strong>July 1863 was the hinge</strong>: Gettysburg (1–3 July, about
    51,000 casualties, the costliest battle ever fought in the western hemisphere) ended Lee's
    second invasion of the North, and Vicksburg's surrender on 4 July cut the Confederacy in
    half along the Mississippi. The <strong>Emancipation Proclamation</strong> (1 January
    1863) redefined the war, brought roughly 180,000 Black soldiers into Union blue — one
    tenth of the Union army by 1865 — and closed the door on European intervention. Then came
    leadership: Grant, who understood that the North could trade casualties and the South
    could not, took command of all Union armies in March 1864 and pinned Lee at Petersburg
    while Sherman marched from Atlanta to the sea, destroying the infrastructure of the
    Deep South. Sherman's capture of Atlanta (September 1864) also did the political work —
    it re-elected Lincoln, who might otherwise have lost to a peace candidate. Lee, starving
    and outflanked, surrendered at Appomattox on 9 April 1865; Lincoln was shot five days
    later.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the North won "just because of numbers". Numbers were
    necessary but not sufficient — they had to be mobilised (drafts, war bonds, a federal
    income tax, mass production), commanded (Grant and Sherman, who spent men where spending
    bought result), and converted into policy (emancipation, which turned the South's
    internal enemy into the Union's asset). The Confederacy came within a few months, in
    1862 and again in 1864, of convincing European powers or northern voters that the war
    was unwinnable. It was won by resources <em>and</em> decisions, not resources alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Did the war change anything?</h2>
  <p>
    On paper, everything. The <strong>13th Amendment</strong> (December 1865) abolished
    slavery — the largest uncompensated transfer of property in history, freeing four million
    people; the <strong>14th</strong> (1868) made citizenship and equal protection
    constitutional and promised that no state could deny them; the <strong>15th</strong>
    (1870) banned racial bars on the vote. Historians call this a "second founding" of the
    Republic: the Constitution now stated what the Declaration had only implied. Reconstruction
    briefly made it real — Black men voted in huge majorities, sat in Congress, built public
    school systems, and served as state legislators across the South under federal troop
    protection. But change was contested by violence and economics: the Ku Klux Klan and
    kindred terror murdered Black political leaders; sharecropping and the Black Codes
    re-created coerced labour under another name; and when northern will failed, the disputed
    election of 1876 was settled by the Compromise of 1877, which withdrew the last federal
    troops. Within a generation the South had written segregation and disenfranchisement into
    law — the Jim Crow system that lasted until the 1960s.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the honest verdict holds both halves: the war changed the Constitution absolutely and
    society far too little. It settled two questions the founders had dodged — no state may
    secede, and no human being may be property — but it left the practical meaning of freedom
    to be fought over again, a century later, by the civil-rights movement. It also forged
    the modern American state: national currency, federal taxation, and the precedent that
    the Union is indissoluble.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The war's human bill: roughly 620,000 soldiers died by the traditional count; a 2011
    demographic study by J. David Hacker put the true total near 750,000 — about 2.5 per cent
    of the entire US population, proportionately equivalent to over 8 million Americans
    today. The South lost a generation of its white male workforce and did not recover its
    pre-war income per head until the twentieth century. The war was also the first "modern"
    war: railways moved armies, the telegraph moved news (and civilians followed casualty
    lists in daily newspapers — a novelty), rifled muskets and ironclad ships made old
    tactics lethal, and both sides fought total war against economies as well as armies,
    culminating in Sherman's deliberate targeting of Southern infrastructure.
  </p>
  <p>
    For exam purposes the deepest debate is over <em>inevitability</em>. Some historians see
    1861 as the unavoidable result of an irrepressible conflict over a labour system; others
    blame failures of politics — a broken party system, extremists on both wings, blunders
    like the Kansas-Nebraska Act — that let a solvable dispute run out of road. The evidence
    fits a middle reading: slavery made the quarrel permanent, but it was the specific
    deadlock of the 1850s — a South that would accept no limits and a North that would accept
    no expansion — that made compromise impossible. Lincoln's genius, in the Gettysburg
    Address, was to rename that deadlock as a test: whether a nation "so conceived and so
    dedicated can long endure". The war's answer, at Appomattox, was yes — at a price the
    founders never imagined.
  </p>

